WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.280
Think of the rack as a system of interfaces.

2
00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:07.120
The mechanical interface locates and supports equipment, but also reserves clearance for

3
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.260
connections and removal.

4
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.820
Electrical interfaces define how power reaches that equipment and what behavior is

5
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.460
permitted during connection, operation and faults.

6
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.180
Thermal interfaces define the conditions under which heat can leave.

7
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.940
Management adds another boundary: what is measured, which controller can request a

8
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.960
change, and who owns the response.

9
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.280
Service ties the interfaces together.

10
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:32.840
A replaceable item isn't practically serviceable if removing it strains an energized

11
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.220
connection or blocks an adjacent cooling circuit.

12
00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:39.100
The figure reveals each boundary separately, then brings them together.

13
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.240
This is a conceptual map, not a wiring drawing.

14
00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.980
Use it to ask a precise question at each handoff: what does

15
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.640
one side provide, what does the other side require, and what evidence

16
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:50.380
shows agreement?

17
00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.480
A shared rack family can reduce integration work, but it doesn't eliminate

18
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.140
these questions.

19
00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.000
The resulting interface review is the organizing tool we'll carry through the

20
00:00:59.040 --> 00:00:59.940
remaining modules.

